I know that sudoku as a replacement for sokoban has been suggested at
least once before, with the main complaints being that it would be
boring, too easy to solve, and not well integrated with the rest of
the game.  The implementation I saw suggested was that the hero is
simply required to drop amounts of gold coins on the sudoku squares,
corresponding to the numerical amounts that complete a sudoku puzzle.

It's time to dust this off and rework it.  First, I envision the
sudoko level as a randomly-generated complement to the sokoban levels,
not as a replacement for them.  Ultimately, other puzzle types could
be added to the game in a random mix, turning the sokoban branch into
a more interesting, general puzzle branch, with each puzzle type given
a nethack twist.

My sudoku implementation would use game objects rather than numbers to
solve the puzzle.  The hero enters the level with it populated with
objects on some squares of the 9x9 grid, these being the starting
clues to solve the puzzle.  For example, an iron skullcap might be on
one square, a banana on another, a potion of fruit juice on another -
9 different objects will eventually be used.  To complete the puzzle,
the hero is required to, first, solve the usual logic of the sudoku.
But to actually complete it, he has to lay down extras of those 9
objects in the appropriate places, one on each empty square.  Thus the
solved puzzle will have 9 copies of the 9 different objects laid down.
The hero has to come up with them from his own inventory, from drops
and finds elsewhere in the dungeon, and if he is lucky, from drops
from monsters that get generated on the sudoku level itself.

There is much room for luck or other nethack factors to play a role in
the difficulty of the puzzle.  If the hero is unlucky or out of his
god's grace, for example, some of the items needed to complete the
sudoko would be rarer items - potion of acid rather than potion of
fruit juice, for example.  The 9 objects should represent each of 9
object types - 1 comestible, 1 potion, 1 tool.  There are more than 9
object types, so really unlucky players might have to come up with 9
of the same type of wand, or 9 of the same type of ring - sudoku might
be a level that is sometimes completed only after the midgame and must
be come back to.

There's much room for tweaking.  The starting objects on the sudoku
spaces might be firmly attached (incapable of being picked up or
moved) or they might be movable and replaced by other starting objects
that the here has more of - again depending on overall game factors.
Or it might be possible for clever players to only destroy a starting
object or teleport it or bury it in order to replace it with another
more favorable one.

Reward for solving sudoku might be access to a magic portal or a door
to a nice treasure.  Speedier solutions (based on game turn
differential or on number of objects dropped/picked up/eliminated)
could yield better rewards.  Thieving monsters or monsters that like
to pick items up and move them around on the level could complicate
things.  Perhaps no carcasses are allowed on a final, solved grid.
Holes created could be a real problem, requiring boulders to fill them
up before solution objects can be placed on those squares.  The grid
may not be strict 9x9 and may have a geometry that needs to be
discovered or dug out.  Doors, water, and lava might be used to
complicate things.

